WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.220
I want to start this video with a&nbsp;
confession. Everything you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:11.430
you know about this channel is a lie.&nbsp;
I, Arran, commonly known as Thoughty2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:17.280
am not real. I am what is known as a deepfake,&nbsp;
a digital construct built from the ground up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.740
for one purpose and one purpose only -&nbsp;
to control your thoughts and opinions...

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.780
...OK, sorry to disappoint any suddenly salivating&nbsp;
sceptics out there - I actually *am* but a boring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:36.000
old meat-and-blood citizen of the United&nbsp;
Kingdom, not a malicious AI that's trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.880
to hijack your brain (although admittedly, if&nbsp;
I actually *was* one I'd definitely deny it.).

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:49.260
Anyway, I made up that strange claim to introduce&nbsp;
you to something called Dead Internet Theory - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:56.280
fringe belief that the internet as we&nbsp;
know it 'died' in 2016, or possibly 2017.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.460
As ridiculous as that may sound, the hypothesis&nbsp;
here is actually kind of interesting. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.080
to Dead Internet theory, sometime in the last&nbsp;
few years the internet was taken over by bots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.460
As a result, the vast majority of&nbsp;
what you see online - forum posts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.400
Instagram accounts, Facebook pages, TikTok&nbsp;
videos - even news headlines - it's all fake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:26.400
Bogus content cooked up by advanced AIs to make&nbsp;
you buy certain things and think certain ways.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.900
As for the occasional real people&nbsp;
you come across online - YouTubers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:37.440
TikTokers, Instagramers, and other so-called&nbsp;
'influencers' - many of us are fake too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.260
and the few that are real are just paid&nbsp;
shills, mouthpieces for big business or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.820
government disinformation units&nbsp;
seeking to influence and control.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.520
In many ways Dead Internet Theory has all the&nbsp;
hallmarks of a ridiculous conspiracy cooked up by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.940
tinfoil hat-wearing cash-buriers on some obscure&nbsp;
online forum. Which is actually no coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.340
because that's exactly where it came from -&nbsp;
the entire idea traces its roots back to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:47.340
single 2021 post on a website called Agora Road&nbsp;
by a user known as illuminatiPirate - obviously.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:52.620
That's hardly a resounding endorsement for&nbsp;
the authenticity of the theory, and yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.420
fast forward a couple of years and people&nbsp;
are still talking about it - the story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.580
even been picked up by the mainstream media. For&nbsp;
whatever reason, despite its dubious origins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.320
something about Dead Internet Theory&nbsp;
just seems to resonate with people.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:11.820
So, here's the million dollar&nbsp;
question - is it actually true?

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:18.420
Let's start by examining the core idea here. This&nbsp;
whole quirky concept is based on the notion that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:24.240
nothing online is quite what it seems. Maybe&nbsp;
you've noticed it yourself... perhaps you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.940
even felt it your entire life, that there's&nbsp;
something wrong with the internet. You don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:36.360
know what it is, but it's there, like a&nbsp;
splinter in your mind, driving you mad.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.220
…OK, so that was actually a mildly&nbsp;
modified Morpheus quote from the Matrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.680
but it could easily have been written for Dead&nbsp;
Internet theory. It all seems to stem from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:52.080
the idea that there's something not quite&nbsp;
right about the internet. That the wacky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.800
creative online world that existed in the late&nbsp;
90s and early 2000s has somehow been replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:05.100
by a bland, homogenous, capitalist machine where&nbsp;
nothing is original and everything is recycled.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.720
You've probably noticed some of this&nbsp;
going on yourself - the same threads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.800
popping up on Reddit, old memes&nbsp;
being revived on an endless loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.900
or the same old arguments being debated on&nbsp;
Twitter and Facebook. Even the news sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:24.360
like a broken record most of the time - another&nbsp;
celebrity’s been canceled, or there's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:30.360
be another blood moon or wolf moon or giant&nbsp;
laser moon tonight that you just can't miss.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.340
According to Dead Internet Theory, the&nbsp;
reason for all this repetition is simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:39.360
almost everything we see online&nbsp;
- the people we interact with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:45.360
the videos we watch, the articles we read,&nbsp;
the forums we contribute to - it's all made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.520
by bots looking to generate clicks&nbsp;
for financial or political gain.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.480
If you've been around long enough to have&nbsp;
surfed the internet on a dial-up modem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.960
you've probably noticed for yourself&nbsp;
that the World Wide Web of today is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.760
very different place to the one you grew up with.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.920
Back in the day, if you weren't MSN'ing&nbsp;
your mates, you were checking out some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.680
weirdo's personal webpage about cuckoo clocks,&nbsp;
watching someone trade a paperclip into a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.400
or buying a few pixels on&nbsp;
the million dollar website.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:21.360
The internet of today is rather more&nbsp;
generic. Despite its unfathomable vastness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:28.860
a staggering 43% of all internet bandwidth is&nbsp;
consumed by the various websites and applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:37.200
owned by just 6 megacorporations - Netflix,&nbsp;
Google, Amazon, Facebook, Microsoft, and Apple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.600
Those wacky personal websites and&nbsp;
blogs have been made redundant by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.320
the rise of Facebook and Instagram,&nbsp;
and many of the more creative ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.340
that have cropped up over the years&nbsp;
have slowly fallen by the wayside to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.860
be replaced with endless online stores and&nbsp;
venture capital-backed social media sites.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.480
Still, let's be honest for a&nbsp;
second here - none of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.200
even remotely convincing evidence&nbsp;
that most of the internet is fake.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:12.180
The internet today feels different because it *is*&nbsp;
different. Like any new technology-cum-cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:19.320
phenomenon it's evolved and matured. The early&nbsp;
internet was built by random basement-dwelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:24.540
gnomes exploring their creativity in a&nbsp;
completely new way. The current internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:30.900
is still built by weirdos *cough* Zuckerberg&nbsp;
*cough* only these oddballs head up some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.400
the biggest companies on the planet. That&nbsp;
means they're more interested in trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:41.400
to satisfy shareholders and pad their bottom&nbsp;
lines than fostering creativity and innovation.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:48.360
As for the recycling of information online,&nbsp;
that's also easy to explain. Reddit threads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.460
pop up again and again because lots of people&nbsp;
with similar thoughts and questions are too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.860
lazy to use the search function, and memes&nbsp;
get recycled because that's just how trends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:03.240
work. We see the same thing in fashion -&nbsp;
certain styles come and go over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.840
but I'm pretty sure nobody thinks&nbsp;
Primark's been taken over by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.880
evil AI because flares are doing the rounds&nbsp;
again. Are flares doing the rounds again?

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.100
The same is true of repeating news stories&nbsp;
- we keep hearing about canceled celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:23.220
because famous people are just normal humans&nbsp;
with thoughts and opions and as we all know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:29.700
thoughts and opinions are now grossly offensive,&nbsp;
and we're constantly being told about blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:34.680
moons and blue moons and purple-spotted moons&nbsp;
because those are cyclical events that occur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:40.680
every few years. None of this means the&nbsp;
internet has died and gone to AI heaven.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.140
But this is where things get interesting.&nbsp;
Because, while Dead Internet theory isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.900
a very good description of the&nbsp;
state of the World Wide Web today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:55.440
there's a pretty good chance it's a scarily&nbsp;
accurate depiction of the very near future.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:01.020
As you probably already know bots are commonplace&nbsp;
online, but you might be surprised to learn just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:06.120
how ubiquitous they really are - in&nbsp;
2021 they accounted for almost half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:12.540
of all internet traffic. Some of these bots&nbsp;
are friendly, like chatbots, web scrapers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:20.040
and that little paperclip from Microsoft Word.&nbsp;
Others - making up a somewhat concerning 27% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:26.076
all internet traffic - are decidedly *un*friendly.&nbsp;
Some are trying to trick you out of your hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.076 --> 00:09:31.560
earned cash, while others want to influence&nbsp;
your thoughts and opinions. Think of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:38.940
malicious bots as the first signs of a cancer&nbsp;
that might one day kill the internet for real.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.580
Of course, we can all spot a bot a mile&nbsp;
away. The awkward, stilted language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:50.520
the inability to respond coherently to&nbsp;
questions, the sparsely populated user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:55.920
profiles with little to no activity... or&nbsp;
at least, that's what most of us assume. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.600
what happens when bots get so advanced that&nbsp;
we can no longer tell human from machine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.480
More importantly, when that day&nbsp;
comes... how will we even know?

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.860
The answer, of course, is that we won't.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:18.300
And that tipping point might come sooner than you&nbsp;
think. Deep learning language models like GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:24.180
are already getting scarily good at producing&nbsp;
human-like responses to almost any question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:29.700
or prompt. LaMDA, a similar application&nbsp;
developed by Google, is so good at acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:35.580
like a human that it famously convinced Google&nbsp;
engineer Blake Lemoine it was actually sentient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:42.000
Rumour has it LaMDA even hired itself a&nbsp;
lawyer to act on its behalf when the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.580
hit the headlines in the summer of 2022.&nbsp;
Just let that sink in for a second - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:52.860
computer program got so good at acting like&nbsp;
a human that it literally *hired legal help*.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:57.540
In other words, the days of recognising&nbsp;
a bot thanks to its poor conversational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:02.520
skills will soon be behind us. Add this&nbsp;
remarkable ability to create believable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:09.480
natural language to the ridiculously impressive&nbsp;
deepfakes and AI generated faces we've all seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:14.460
doing the rounds recently - this person&nbsp;
doesn’t exist by the way, or this person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:20.580
or this one - and it's only a matter of&nbsp;
time before bots will be capable of so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:25.140
convincingly impersonating real people&nbsp;
that we'll never know the difference.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:32.700
We humans have to spend years eating, growing,&nbsp;
and trying to convince people to have sex with us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:38.040
before we're able to make copies of ourselves.&nbsp;
Our numbers are constrained by food supplies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:43.020
housing, and access to clean water&nbsp;
and medicine. Bots, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:49.440
have no such worries - their numbers are&nbsp;
essentially limitless, and it isn‘t hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:56.040
to imagine a world where there are millions,&nbsp;
even billions of bots to every human online.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:03.960
In 2019, Facebook claimed to have shut&nbsp;
down a frankly ridiculous 5.4 billion fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:09.660
accounts - more than twice the number of actual&nbsp;
users on the site at the time. It's the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:14.460
story on every other social media platform too&nbsp;
- Elon Musk tried to pull out of buying Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:20.940
because he believed at least 20% of all accounts&nbsp;
on the site were fake. The vast majority of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.860
bogus accounts are incredibly basic - they're&nbsp;
created en-masse by cheap software so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:31.380
recognise them as fakes a mile off. But imagine&nbsp;
a world where those accounts are populated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:38.760
AI-generated portraits and deepfakes backed&nbsp;
up by a natural language program like GPT-3.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:43.200
In the era of fake news, we're all&nbsp;
too familiar with the idea that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.280
everything we see online can be trusted -&nbsp;
that there are people out there who want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:52.020
to make us think certain things or behave&nbsp;
in certain ways to suit their own agendas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:57.840
from fake reviews on Amazon right up to the&nbsp;
Cambridge Analytica scandal, where vast amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.820
of Facebook user data was harvested to help&nbsp;
sway voters in US Presidential Elections.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:04.980
As bots get more convincing, it's an&nbsp;
absolute certainty that people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:10.020
try and use them to gain money, power,&nbsp;
or both, and that will inevitably mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:16.080
a huge influx of bad-bots generating fake&nbsp;
content to sway public opinion and garner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:23.100
artificial engagement. Cambridge Analytica used&nbsp;
Facebook data to create micro-targeted ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:32.100
but the next step might be micro-targeted bots -&nbsp;
an artificial construct that shares its target's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:38.700
interests and belief systems, perhaps even with&nbsp;
AI-generated physical characteristics that play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:44.520
up to the target‘s sexual preferences. These&nbsp;
bots could have realistic conversations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:50.220
millions of unsuspecting marks online,&nbsp;
to influence their political opinions or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:55.020
coax money from them. And when you literally&nbsp;
can’t tell if who you’re talking to is a bot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:01.680
how do you even begin to stop something like that?
Once bots like these become truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:07.140
indistinguishable from real humans and cheap and&nbsp;
accessible enough that bad actors can spread them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:14.220
far and wide, the internet as we know it&nbsp;
really may come under existential threat.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:19.020
What's really scary is, the more&nbsp;
you think about all of this stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:25.440
the more inevitable it starts to feel. The&nbsp;
technology *will* get good enough to fool us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:30.960
and there *will* be people willing to&nbsp;
use that to achieve their own ends.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.600
OK, it's time to address the elephant&nbsp;
in the room here. On the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:41.340
I'm telling you that the internet isn't dead yet,&nbsp;
and that all these problems lie in humanity’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:45.480
future. On the other hand, I'm telling you&nbsp;
that there will come a time when we can no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:53.400
longer distinguish real users from bots. So...&nbsp;
how do I know that time hasn't already come?

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:58.740
The truth is, I don't. It's impossible&nbsp;
to say for absolutely certain that more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.880
advanced versions of the technologies I've&nbsp;
talked about in this video aren't already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:09.420
being put to use by bad actors-unknown, but as&nbsp;
of today it seems pretty unlikely. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:15.360
the kind of bots required to truly fool people&nbsp;
simply don't exist yet on the free market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:21.600
so the only people who could possibly have&nbsp;
developed them are world governments or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:26.100
major tech companies. In other words, for&nbsp;
Dead Internet Theory to be true today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:33.360
it would require a global conspiracy at the&nbsp;
very highest level, and that seems unlikely.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.960
Still, the rise of bots is a serious threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:41.940
and both big business and world governments are&nbsp;
taking it incredibly seriously. Several tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:46.440
firms - including Microsoft - are already&nbsp;
working on software to detect deepfakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:53.220
and various high profile computer scientists are&nbsp;
trying to figure out ways to embed watermarks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.220 --> 00:16:00.840
output text from programs like GPT-3. You could&nbsp;
say we‘re arming ourselves for a future war.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:05.820
Because Dead Internet Theory has&nbsp;
had the wrong name all along.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.020
It isn’t a theory - it’s a prophecy.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:14.160
Thanks for watching.

